      {
        "id": "s5_bowmans_capsule",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Bowman's capsule",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Bowman's capsule",
        "why": "The capsule and the tuft of capillaries inside it work as one sieve, which is why blood can be filtered without any energy being spent.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Calling the glomerulus part of the tubule. It is a tuft of capillaries.",
          "Leaving out podocytes and filtration slits."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "A cup around a knot of capillaries: capsule plus glomerulus is the sieve.",
        "margin_note": "The nephron has two parts. This is the first.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Structure of the nephron",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "A nephron has 2 parts: Bowman's capsule",
          "and the renal tubule.",
          {
            "text": "1. Bowman's capsule",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It lies in the cortex and is a double",
          "walled cup.",
          "Its inner wall has cells called podocytes,",
          "which wrap around each capillary and leave",
          "minute spaces called filtration slits.",
          "The capsule encloses a tuft of capillaries",
          "called the glomerulus.",
          "The glomerulus and the inner wall together",
          "form a sieve.",
          "Blood enters by the afferent arteriole and",
          "leaves by the efferent arteriole."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s6_pct_henle",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "PCT and loop of Henle",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "PCT and Henle's loop",
        "why": "The brush border of the proximal tubule is a huge surface for taking back what the body needs, and the hairpin loop is what lets urine be concentrated.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Placing the loop of Henle in the cortex. It runs into the medulla.",
          "Forgetting that the ascending limb is thin then thick."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "PCT in the cortex, the hairpin dips into the medulla.",
        "margin_note": "Three segments in the tubule. Take them in order.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "2. Renal tubule",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It has 3 segments: PCT, Henle's loop, DCT.",
          {
            "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It lies in the cortex, is wide and highly",
          "coiled, and is lined by simple cuboidal",
          "epithelium with a brush border.",
          {
            "text": "Henle's loop",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It lies in the medulla and is hairpin",
          "shaped, with a descending and an ascending",
          "limb.",
          "The proximal part of the ascending limb is",
          "thin and the distal part is thick.",
          "The thick ascending limb continues into the",
          "DCT."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s7_dct_collecting",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "DCT and collecting duct",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "DCT and collecting duct",
        "why": "Many nephrons pour into one duct, so the last stretch of the path is shared and ends at a single opening in the renal papilla.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that each nephron has its own duct to the pelvis. Many share one.",
          "Leaving out the duct of Bellini."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "DCT to initial duct to collecting duct to duct of Bellini.",
        "margin_note": "End at the renal papilla. That is where the urine leaves.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Distal convoluted tubule",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It lies in the cortex and continues as the",
          "initial collecting duct.",
          "Some initial collecting ducts join to form",
          "a straight collecting duct, which passes",
          "through the medullary pyramid.",
          "In the medulla the tubes of each pyramid",
          "join and form the duct of Bellini, which",
          "opens at the tip of the renal papilla."
        ]
      },
